            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Understanding .NET Project Structure</h2>
                    <p>Every .NET project follows a consistent structure. Understanding these files and folders will help you navigate any .NET project and configure your applications correctly.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Anatomy of a .NET Project</h2>
                    <p>When you create a new console application, you get this structure:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash">MyApp/
├── MyApp.csproj          # Project file
├── Program.cs            # Entry point
├── bin/                  # Compiled output
│   └── Debug/
│       └── net8.0/
└── obj/                  # Intermediate build files
    └── Debug/
        └── net8.0/</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>The Project File (.csproj)</h2>
                    <p>The <code>.csproj</code> file is the heart of your project. It defines the target framework, dependencies, and build settings:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk"&gt;

  &lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
    &lt;OutputType&gt;Exe&lt;/OutputType&gt;
    &lt;TargetFramework&gt;net8.0&lt;/TargetFramework&gt;
    &lt;ImplicitUsings&gt;enable&lt;/ImplicitUsings&gt;
    &lt;Nullable&gt;enable&lt;/Nullable&gt;
  &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;

&lt;/Project&gt;</code></pre>

                    <h3>Key Elements Explained</h3>
                    <div class="property-grid">
                        <div class="property-card">
                            <code>Sdk</code>
                            <p>The SDK type. <code>Microsoft.NET.Sdk</code> for libraries/console, <code>Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web</code> for web apps.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="property-card">
                            <code>OutputType</code>
                            <p><code>Exe</code> for executables, <code>Library</code> for class libraries.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="property-card">
                            <code>TargetFramework</code>
                            <p>The .NET version to target (e.g., <code>net8.0</code>, <code>net9.0</code>).</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="property-card">
                            <code>ImplicitUsings</code>
                            <p>Automatically includes common using statements.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="property-card">
                            <code>Nullable</code>
                            <p>Enables nullable reference type analysis.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Common Project Properties</h2>
                    <p>You can configure many aspects of your project in the <code>.csproj</code> file:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk"&gt;

  &lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
    &lt;OutputType&gt;Exe&lt;/OutputType&gt;
    &lt;TargetFramework&gt;net8.0&lt;/TargetFramework&gt;
    &lt;ImplicitUsings&gt;enable&lt;/ImplicitUsings&gt;
    &lt;Nullable&gt;enable&lt;/Nullable&gt;
    
    &lt;!-- Assembly information --&gt;
    &lt;AssemblyName&gt;MyApplication&lt;/AssemblyName&gt;
    &lt;RootNamespace&gt;MyCompany.MyApp&lt;/RootNamespace&gt;
    &lt;Version&gt;1.0.0&lt;/Version&gt;
    &lt;Authors&gt;Your Name&lt;/Authors&gt;
    &lt;Company&gt;Your Company&lt;/Company&gt;
    &lt;Description&gt;A description of your app&lt;/Description&gt;
    
    &lt;!-- Build settings --&gt;
    &lt;LangVersion&gt;latest&lt;/LangVersion&gt;
    &lt;TreatWarningsAsErrors&gt;true&lt;/TreatWarningsAsErrors&gt;
  &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;

&lt;/Project&gt;</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Multi-Targeting</h2>
                    <p>You can target multiple .NET versions from a single project:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk"&gt;

  &lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
    &lt;!-- Note: TargetFrameworks (plural) --&gt;
    &lt;TargetFrameworks&gt;net6.0;net7.0;net8.0&lt;/TargetFrameworks&gt;
  &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;

&lt;/Project&gt;</code></pre>

                    <p>This is useful for libraries that need to support multiple .NET versions.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Package References</h2>
                    <p>NuGet packages are listed in the project file:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk"&gt;

  &lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
    &lt;TargetFramework&gt;net8.0&lt;/TargetFramework&gt;
  &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;

  &lt;ItemGroup&gt;
    &lt;PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" Version="13.0.3" /&gt;
    &lt;PackageReference Include="Serilog" Version="3.1.1" /&gt;
    &lt;PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" Version="8.0.0" /&gt;
  &lt;/ItemGroup&gt;

&lt;/Project&gt;</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Tip</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>Use <code>dotnet add package PackageName</code> to add packages instead of editing the file manually.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Project References</h2>
                    <p>Reference other projects in your solution:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;ItemGroup&gt;
  &lt;ProjectReference Include="..\MyLibrary\MyLibrary.csproj" /&gt;
  &lt;ProjectReference Include="..\SharedCode\SharedCode.csproj" /&gt;
&lt;/ItemGroup&gt;</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Solution Files (.sln)</h2>
                    <p>Solution files organize multiple projects together. They're especially important for:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Visual Studio compatibility</li>
                        <li>Building multiple projects at once</li>
                        <li>Organizing large codebases</li>
                    </ul>

                    <p>A typical solution structure:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash">MySolution/
├── MySolution.sln
├── src/
│   ├── MyApp/
│   │   ├── MyApp.csproj
│   │   └── Program.cs
│   └── MyLibrary/
│       ├── MyLibrary.csproj
│       └── Calculator.cs
└── tests/
    └── MyApp.Tests/
        ├── MyApp.Tests.csproj
        └── CalculatorTests.cs</code></pre>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Create this structure
dotnet new sln -n MySolution
mkdir src tests
dotnet new console -n MyApp -o src/MyApp
dotnet new classlib -n MyLibrary -o src/MyLibrary
dotnet new xunit -n MyApp.Tests -o tests/MyApp.Tests

# Add projects to solution
dotnet sln add src/MyApp/MyApp.csproj
dotnet sln add src/MyLibrary/MyLibrary.csproj
dotnet sln add tests/MyApp.Tests/MyApp.Tests.csproj</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>The bin and obj Folders</h2>
                    
                    <h3>bin/ (Binary Output)</h3>
                    <p>Contains the compiled output of your project:</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">bin/
├── Debug/
│   └── net8.0/
│       ├── MyApp.dll          # Your compiled code
│       ├── MyApp.exe          # Executable (Windows)
│       ├── MyApp.deps.json    # Dependency information
│       ├── MyApp.runtimeconfig.json
│       └── MyApp.pdb          # Debug symbols
└── Release/
    └── net8.0/
        └── ...</code></pre>

                    <h3>obj/ (Intermediate Files)</h3>
                    <p>Contains intermediate build artifacts. You generally don't need to look here, but it includes:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Compiler cache files</li>
                        <li>Generated source files</li>
                        <li>NuGet restore outputs</li>
                    </ul>

                    <div class="warning-box">
                        <div class="warning-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <path d="M10.29 3.86L1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0z"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="9" x2="12" y2="13"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="17" x2="12.01" y2="17"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Git Ignore</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>Always add <code>bin/</code> and <code>obj/</code> to your <code>.gitignore</code>. These folders are regenerated during build.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Global.json</h2>
                    <p>Pin the SDK version for your project or solution:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-json">{
  "sdk": {
    "version": "8.0.100",
    "rollForward": "latestFeature"
  }
}</code></pre>

                    <p>Place this file in your solution root to ensure everyone uses the same SDK version.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Directory.Build.props</h2>
                    <p>Share settings across multiple projects:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;!-- Directory.Build.props in solution root --&gt;
&lt;Project&gt;
  &lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
    &lt;TargetFramework&gt;net8.0&lt;/TargetFramework&gt;
    &lt;ImplicitUsings&gt;enable&lt;/ImplicitUsings&gt;
    &lt;Nullable&gt;enable&lt;/Nullable&gt;
    &lt;TreatWarningsAsErrors&gt;true&lt;/TreatWarningsAsErrors&gt;
    &lt;Company&gt;My Company&lt;/Company&gt;
  &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;
&lt;/Project&gt;</code></pre>

                    <p>All projects in subdirectories will inherit these settings automatically.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
                    <div class="takeaways">
                        <LessonTakeaway>The <code>.csproj</code> file defines your project's settings, dependencies, and build configuration.</LessonTakeaway>
                        <LessonTakeaway>Solution files (<code>.sln</code>) organize multiple projects together.</LessonTakeaway>
                        <LessonTakeaway>Use <code>global.json</code> to pin SDK versions for consistency.</LessonTakeaway>
                        <LessonTakeaway>Use <code>Directory.Build.props</code> to share settings across projects.</LessonTakeaway>
                    </div>
                </section>
            </div>

